istently show Blackburn leading by double digits", "Prediction markets give Green only a 4-6% chance; independent bids fragment the field without threatening the lead"], "rationale": "Every strand of evidence points the same way: Tennessee's deep Republican lean, unanimous Safe/Solid R ratings, polling averages around a dozen points for Blackburn, and markets pricing Green near one-in-twenty. The open seat and a crowded independent field add noise but no credible path for the Democrat.", "watch": "A consolidated anti-Blackburn coalition behind Green or a major late scandal could narrow the margin, but nothing in recent coverage suggests either is developing.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Bill Lee (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Tennessee is among the most Republican-leaning states holding a governor's race this year, and the open seat left by term-limited Bill Lee has not changed that. Senator Marsha Blackburn enters the general election with strong name recognition, while Democrat Jerri Green is a lesser-known challenger campaigning on affordability and against the Republican supermajority. Polls since spring have consistently shown Blackburn ahead by double digits, though the most recent survey put the gap closer to nine points.\n\nIndependent candidates, including Lauren Pinkston, are drawing attention and prompted requests from the Green campaign to consolidate, but there is little evidence they threaten Blackburn's lead. Every major race rating calls the contest Safe or Solid Republican, and prediction markets give Green roughly a one-in-twenty chance. Blackburn has declined to debate, and no recent development suggests the race is competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0257, "margin": -11.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0463, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0186, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0193, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0323, "margin"